title a runner is a file the pier executes, so the population is a union
prompt the baton -- take the next agent-doable lap, reds first, in the amphora and bron-resins lane
rota lap 4281, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; its fixed seat is the pull test, and pulling the shared-pen population rule showed a shebang-only swap would take 2,424 files with it
think REDS %601 was mine and OPEN, and its named remainder was exact -- a guard reading tracked runners for a fixed /tmp/<name> write.
think Before building, I asked whether the work already stood, which is the lesson Bakery's superseded stash put on the card. It did: shared_pen_scan.sh is that guard, and my own last lap widened it from git ls-files 'tools/*' to .sh and .rish.
think So the remainder was not a missing guard but a population picked twice by a choice wearing a rule's clothes -- first a room, then a suffix. A runner is a file the pier executes.
think Measured seven tracked runners outside the suffix population. Three are tools/hooks/{commit-msg,pre-commit,post-commit}, which core.hooksPath runs on every ship at every commit -- the exact instant %601 describes. A fourth is tools/m/mind-bin/git.
think Before swapping the rule I measured what a swap would cost: 2,424 of 3,298 suffixed sources carry no shebang, because they are sourced or run by a named interpreter. Shebang-only buys 7 and drops 2,424. So the population is a union, never a swap, and the control now proves that leg by name.
think The fast reading was corroborated against a slow one -- git grep -I -n keeping lineno == 1 against a hand loop reading the first two bytes of every tracked file. Both answer 881 and name the same seven, which is the check worth having when a fast reading replaces a slow one.
obs The seven hold no pen today, so both ceilings stand unmoved at 46 files and 15 wiping. Reach bought, number unchanged -- the same honest shape the tools/ widening took one lap earlier.
obs Named against myself: the card says hold still while the cold roster runs, and I edited files while it ran. The pass closed tree_moved=yes and withheld its receipt, so its reading is informative rather than authoritative. The hot pass is what this round stands on.
obs The cold pass surfaced a red that was not mine and was agent-closable: REDS-fold-recital.md line 520 named row %614 while linking the shelf whose own row reads %613. Repaired by reading the shelf rather than inferring; reds_citation GREEN, fold_disagree 1 -> 0.
obs stash_record stays red at orphans=12, all 12 fold shelves on no ref -- %592's standing seat, not this lap's.
obs The rebase brought a peer's %619, which reads my own row back to me: the sentence in %601 setting this lap aside is the pointer that sent two ships at one guard inside an hour. A pointer with no claim beside it invites duplication, addressed to everyone. That peer duplicated my LAST lap's widening and dropped theirs; this lap's union is beyond what they had, and the rebase applied it cleanly, which is the check that says so.
obs The pin went over bound after the rebase -- two peer rows plus my edit, 41,735 against 40,960. %601 is BOOKED and BOOKED may fold, so I folded my own row rather than shaving a peer's: 38,186, monotone and citation witnesses both GREEN.
obs A find for Keaton, measured rather than argued: reds_spine_derive reads published_doubles=2 while derived-spine.md names %530 alone. The second is %592, bound to stamps 20260907.211709 and 20260907.215114, both folded to shelves. The law says such a pair waits on his word, so I surfaced it on the card rather than editing the law.
